GO ASSIGNMENT GAP SIZED: 6 SITES ACROSS 9 REPOS. DO NOT BUILD IT.

Instrument in-repo at scripts/measure-go-receiver-typing-gap.py, positive-controlled
before use (a synthetic fixture with four known assigned sites; the control caught a real
scanner bug first -- tree-sitter-go spells a type-position `pkg.Name` as `qualified_type`,
not `selector_expression`, so every composite-literal constructor was being dropped and
the fixture reported 3 of 4).

Corpus: caddy, prometheus, alertmanager, kserve, cosign, act, grype, dnscrypt-proxy,
gocryptfs.

  ASSIGNED candidate sites                                   143
    blocked today (method name in ambiguous_names)           107
    NO boundary reported anywhere today                       27
      reachable by the ASSIGNMENT fix                          6   <- the answer
      rooted at a COMPOSITE LITERAL, so it is NOT               21

THE DENOMINATOR DISTINCTION THAT DOES THE WORK. 116 of the 143 sites have a constructor
that is ITSELF a catalogued row (exec.Command, net.Dial, smtp.Dial ...), so the repo
already reports a boundary at that line. For those the method call is a second signal at
the sink, not a missing crossing. Quoting 143, or even 107, as "the gap" would oversell
it by 24x / 18x. The residual value in that bucket is narrow but real: a taint that
enters AFTER construction (cmd.Args = append(cmd.Args, userInput); cmd.Run()) is only
caught at the method call. Not measured, and not claimed.

THE DOMINANT ROW IS NOT AN ASSIGNMENT GAP AT ALL, which is why the split mattered.
net/http.Client.Do is 17 of the 27. With the positive control the earlier probe on this
thread was missing (http.Get tags, so net/http is reachable):

  (&http.Client{}).Do(r)          INLINE    -> 0 boundaries
  c := &http.Client{}; c.Do(r)    ASSIGNED  -> 0 boundaries

Unreachable in BOTH forms, because a composite literal offers no constructor CALL to
type from. That is a composite-literal receiver-typing gap, a different mechanism.
(This supersedes the "OPEN and unmeasured" note on this thread about whether
net/http.Client.Do is reachable -- it is not, in any form, and now has a control.)

The asked-about gap is confirmed cleanly and separately on slog, where the constructor IS
a call:  l := slog.Default(); l.Info(m) -> 0   vs   slog.Default().Info(m) -> 1.

WHAT TO BUILD INSTEAD, ranked by measured value rather than by novelty:

  1. COMPOSITE-LITERAL receiver typing -- 21 sites, and the only security-relevant one.
     `client := &http.Client{}; client.Do(req)` with a tainted URL is SSRF and is
     invisible today in every syntactic form. net_send, not logging.
  2. PARAM shape (typed parameter, `func h(c *gin.Context)`) -- 653 candidates, 145
     blocked. Technically the easiest (Go parameters carry an explicit type, nothing to
     infer) but the payoff is poor: ~136 of the 145 blocked are
     slog.Logger.{Info,Error,Warn,Debug}, i.e. the `logging` boundary. The headline 653
     is dominated by testing.T.TempDir, which ALREADY tags by short name.
  3. The ASSIGNMENT gap itself -- 6 sites, 5 slog + 1 gin.Engine.Run. Same territory as
     the python "B" mechanism that was measured at zero and recorded DO NOT BUILD.

CAVEATS, load-bearing. The constructor->type map is HAND-BUILT from Go stdlib semantics,
because the catalogue records which METHODS are I/O and not which functions RETURN the
receiving type; a missing row undercounts. Nine repos, so this is a corpus statement and
not a language one. And these are static-scan SITES, not production boundary CHAINS --
the python work established those are different populations (128 sites vs 125 chains),
so do not compare this number against a chain count without re-deriving it.
